1
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:10,965
This is RoBERTa, a language model with about 125 million learned numbers.

2
00:00:11,005 --> 00:00:14,822
To teach it a new task, the obvious move is to adjust all of them.

3
00:00:14,862 --> 00:00:17,560
FrameFT adjusts 24,000.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,201
That's 0.02% of the model.

5
00:00:20,241 --> 00:00:24,406
And on language benchmarks, it matches, or even beats, full fine-tuning.

6
00:00:25,253 --> 00:00:29,960
In this video, we'll walk through the whole process: the problem FrameFT solves,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,885
what a frame actually is, how the frame gets built,

8
00:00:32,925 --> 00:00:36,426
how training works, and why the code makes the choices it does.

9
00:00:36,466 --> 00:00:36,847
Then,

10
00:00:36,887 --> 00:00:41,275
an experiment we're running to test whether the frame itself is what makes it work.

11
00:00:42,417 --> 00:00:46,950
Most of what a model knows lives in weight matrices: big grids of numbers.

12
00:00:46,990 --> 00:00:50,797
A layer takes an input vector, multiplies it by its matrix,

13
00:00:50,837 --> 00:00:52,716
and produces an output vector.

14
00:00:52,756 --> 00:01:00,087
In RoBERTa-base, a typical matrix is 768 by 768, which is almost 600,000 numbers.

15
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:03,251
Fine-tuning means learning a change to each matrix.

16
00:01:03,291 --> 00:01:07,316
We'll call that change ΔW, where delta just means "change in".

17
00:01:07,356 --> 00:01:10,271
The new weights are the old weights plus ΔW.

18
00:01:10,311 --> 00:01:14,587
The old weights stay frozen, and all of the learning goes into ΔW.

19
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:17,223
If ΔW can be any matrix at all,

20
00:01:17,263 --> 00:01:20,723
we're back to training hundreds of thousands of numbers per matrix,

21
00:01:20,763 --> 00:01:24,098
and storing a whole new copy of the model for every task.

22
00:01:24,138 --> 00:01:29,105
So the real question is: how can we describe ΔW with far fewer numbers?

23
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:35,225
Methods that answer this are called parameter-efficient fine-tuning, or PEFT.

24
00:01:35,265 --> 00:01:36,690
The best known is LoRA.

25
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:41,102
It writes ΔW as a tall, thin matrix times a short, wide one.

26
00:01:41,142 --> 00:01:43,791
The thin side is called the rank: roughly,

27
00:01:43,831 --> 00:01:47,185
how many independent patterns ΔW can contain.

28
00:01:47,225 --> 00:01:52,439
At rank eight, that's about 12,000 numbers per matrix, instead of 600,000.

29
00:01:53,283 --> 00:01:55,341
There's another way to be economical.

30
00:01:55,381 --> 00:01:58,097
Choose a fixed dictionary of patterns ahead of time,

31
00:01:58,137 --> 00:02:00,915
and learn only how much of each pattern to use.

32
00:02:00,955 --> 00:02:02,988
Those amounts are called coefficients.

33
00:02:03,028 --> 00:02:06,612
It's the same trick JPEG uses to compress a photo:

34
00:02:06,652 --> 00:02:10,761
it stores the amounts of simple wave patterns, instead of every pixel.

35
00:02:11,617 --> 00:02:16,858
FourierFT, one of the paper's baselines, uses Fourier waves as its dictionary.

36
00:02:16,898 --> 00:02:20,877
FrameFT uses something more general, called a tight fusion frame.

37
00:02:20,917 --> 00:02:23,548
To see what that is, we'll build up three ideas:

38
00:02:23,588 --> 00:02:26,222
a basis, a frame, and a fusion frame.

39
00:02:27,383 --> 00:02:28,699
Start with a basis.

40
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:31,376
In a flat plane, two arrows at right angles,

41
00:02:31,416 --> 00:02:34,295
each of length one, form an orthonormal basis.

42
00:02:34,335 --> 00:02:38,229
Any vector is some amount of the first arrow plus some amount of the second,

43
00:02:38,269 --> 00:02:43,000
and those amounts are just its shadows on each arrow, also called dot products.

44
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:47,311
A nice bonus: the squared shadows add up to the squared length of the vector.

45
00:02:47,351 --> 00:02:48,728
Nothing is lost.

46
00:02:48,883 --> 00:02:52,957
A frame relaxes one rule: you may use more arrows than dimensions.

47
00:02:52,997 --> 00:02:56,632
Here are three arrows, evenly spaced, in a two-dimensional plane.

48
00:02:56,672 --> 00:02:58,908
Every vector can still be built from them.

49
00:02:58,948 --> 00:03:03,637
There's just more than one way to do it, and that extra slack is called redundancy.

50
00:03:03,783 --> 00:03:06,820
A frame is tight if it treats every direction equally.

51
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:10,248
Watch the three squared shadows as the vector spins around.

52
00:03:10,288 --> 00:03:13,994
Each one rises and falls, but their sum never changes:

53
00:03:14,034 --> 00:03:17,152
it's always exactly one and a half times the squared length.

54
00:03:17,192 --> 00:03:20,772
Now compare a lopsided frame, with the arrows bunched together.

55
00:03:20,812 --> 00:03:24,776
The total wobbles, because some directions get more attention than others.

56
00:03:24,816 --> 00:03:26,732
Tightness buys something practical.

57
00:03:26,772 --> 00:03:30,495
To rebuild a vector, measure its shadows, then add the arrows back,

58
00:03:30,535 --> 00:03:33,943
weighted by those shadows, and divide by that constant.

59
00:03:33,983 --> 00:03:35,440
Measure, then rebuild.

60
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:38,715
Frame theory calls these two steps analysis and synthesis,

61
00:03:38,755 --> 00:03:41,858
and both will show up again inside FrameFT.

62
00:03:43,015 --> 00:03:45,322
A fusion frame goes one step further.

63
00:03:45,362 --> 00:03:49,197
Instead of single arrows, its building blocks are whole subspaces,

64
00:03:49,237 --> 00:03:51,653
like lines and planes through the origin.

65
00:03:51,693 --> 00:03:53,462
And instead of a shadow on an arrow,

66
00:03:53,502 --> 00:03:56,995
you take the shadow on each subspace, called a projection.

67
00:03:58,482 --> 00:04:01,468
The fusion frame is tight when the squared lengths of all the

68
00:04:01,508 --> 00:04:05,864
projections always add up to the same multiple of the vector's squared length.

69
00:04:05,904 --> 00:04:10,263
Here, a plane and a perpendicular line split three-dimensional space.

70
00:04:10,303 --> 00:04:12,390
The two shadows obey Pythagoras:

71
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:15,544
together they account for the whole vector, however it points.

72
00:04:16,615 --> 00:04:20,021
This is exactly the kind of frame FrameFT's code builds.

73
00:04:20,061 --> 00:04:21,648
The redundancy is one,

74
00:04:21,688 --> 00:04:25,882
so the subspaces are perpendicular and fit together with no overlap.

75
00:04:25,922 --> 00:04:32,884
For RoBERTa, the 768-dimensional space is carved into 384 perpendicular planes.

76
00:04:32,924 --> 00:04:36,116
Stack the frame's vectors as rows, two per plane,

77
00:04:36,156 --> 00:04:39,892
and you get a 768 by 768 matrix called B.

78
00:04:39,932 --> 00:04:44,448
It's an orthogonal matrix: its rows are perpendicular and of length one,

79
00:04:44,488 --> 00:04:47,400
so it can rotate space, but never stretches it.

80
00:04:48,548 --> 00:04:49,823
How is B built?

81
00:04:49,863 --> 00:04:53,416
A key design choice: B is never trained, and never saved.

82
00:04:53,456 --> 00:04:55,745
It's regenerated from just two numbers,

83
00:04:55,785 --> 00:05:00,743
the size n and the subspace dimension l, and it comes out identical every time.

84
00:05:00,783 --> 00:05:04,019
So one copy can be shared by every layer of that size.

85
00:05:04,881 --> 00:05:06,778
The construction has three steps.

86
00:05:06,818 --> 00:05:09,100
Step one is called Spectral Tetris.

87
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:13,010
It builds a small seed matrix whose columns all have length one,

88
00:05:13,050 --> 00:05:16,470
and whose rows are perpendicular and carry equal energy.

89
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:19,160
It fills each row with ones, from left to right.

90
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:23,649
When a row needs a fractional amount to finish, it drops in a two by two block,

91
00:05:23,689 --> 00:05:27,986
the tetris piece, which splits the leftover perfectly between two rows.

92
00:05:28,026 --> 00:05:31,904
In FrameFT's settings the sizes always divide evenly,

93
00:05:31,944 --> 00:05:34,517
so the seed is simply a staircase of ones.

94
00:05:35,181 --> 00:05:38,854
Step two is modulation, and it uses complex numbers,

95
00:05:38,894 --> 00:05:42,698
which you can picture as arrows, where multiplying means rotating.

96
00:05:42,738 --> 00:05:44,497
Make many copies of the seed.

97
00:05:44,537 --> 00:05:49,315
In each copy, rotate every column by an angle that grows steadily along the row,

98
00:05:49,355 --> 00:05:54,116
and give every copy its own speed, like clock hands spinning at different rates.

99
00:05:54,156 --> 00:05:56,449
Compare two copies with different speeds,

100
00:05:56,489 --> 00:06:00,119
and the rotations cancel out, so the copies are perpendicular.

101
00:06:00,159 --> 00:06:03,882
It's the same cancellation that makes the Fourier transform work.

102
00:06:04,648 --> 00:06:08,719
Step three: neural networks use real numbers, not complex ones.

103
00:06:08,759 --> 00:06:11,656
So each complex number, a plus i b,

104
00:06:11,696 --> 00:06:13,831
is replaced by a two by two block of real

105
00:06:13,871 --> 00:06:17,479
numbers that rotates and scales in exactly the same way.

106
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:24,144
This doubles the size, turning 384 complex coordinates into 768 real ones.

107
00:06:24,781 --> 00:06:26,699
Here's the result for RoBERTa:

108
00:06:26,739 --> 00:06:30,012
a 768 by 768 orthogonal matrix

109
00:06:30,052 --> 00:06:34,388
whose rows are cosine and sine waves of increasing frequency.

110
00:06:34,428 --> 00:06:38,838
At these settings, the tight fusion frame is a real-valued Fourier basis.

111
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:41,298
Now the main idea.

112
00:06:41,338 --> 00:06:47,066
FrameFT writes the update as B transpose, times S, times B, times a scale factor.

113
00:06:47,106 --> 00:06:48,691
B is the frozen frame.

114
00:06:48,731 --> 00:06:53,866
S is a 768 by 768 grid that's almost entirely zeros.

115
00:06:53,906 --> 00:06:56,689
Only a thousand of its entries may be nonzero,

116
00:06:56,729 --> 00:07:01,610
and those thousand numbers are the only things FrameFT trains for this matrix.

117
00:07:01,747 --> 00:07:03,775
Read it from the input's point of view.

118
00:07:03,815 --> 00:07:08,302
First, B transpose measures the input's shadows on every frame direction.

119
00:07:08,342 --> 00:07:09,620
That's analysis.

120
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:14,065
Then S edits those measurements, scaling some, and mixing a few into others.

121
00:07:14,105 --> 00:07:18,208
Finally, B turns the edited measurements back into an ordinary vector.

122
00:07:18,248 --> 00:07:19,487
That's synthesis.

123
00:07:19,527 --> 00:07:21,082
Measure, edit, rebuild.

124
00:07:21,847 --> 00:07:25,648
Another way to see it: each coefficient owns one fixed pattern,

125
00:07:25,688 --> 00:07:31,023
called an outer product: one frame row stood on its end, times another lying flat.

126
00:07:31,063 --> 00:07:33,992
Frame rows are waves spread across the whole vector,

127
00:07:34,032 --> 00:07:36,877
so each pattern covers the entire matrix.

128
00:07:36,917 --> 00:07:41,387
A single coefficient nudges nearly all 600,000 weights at once,

129
00:07:41,427 --> 00:07:47,031
in a coordinated wave, and ΔW is simply the sum of a thousand such patterns.

130
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:51,208
Where do the thousand positions go?

131
00:07:51,248 --> 00:07:54,024
They're picked at random, once, using a seed:

132
00:07:54,064 --> 00:07:56,549
the starting number for a random number generator,

133
00:07:56,589 --> 00:07:59,382
so the same seed always makes the same choices.

134
00:07:59,422 --> 00:08:03,703
Because the seed can regenerate them, the positions never need to be saved.

135
00:08:03,743 --> 00:08:08,282
And with the share-entry option used here, every layer uses the same positions.

136
00:08:09,447 --> 00:08:10,838
Two knobs shape S.

137
00:08:10,878 --> 00:08:12,679
The first is the block size.

138
00:08:12,719 --> 00:08:18,501
With a block size of 768, used for MRPC, a coefficient can sit anywhere,

139
00:08:18,541 --> 00:08:20,852
linking any frame direction to any other.

140
00:08:20,892 --> 00:08:23,898
With a block size of two, used for RTE,

141
00:08:23,938 --> 00:08:27,799
coefficients may only sit in the two by two blocks along the diagonal.

142
00:08:27,839 --> 00:08:32,678
Each block links one plane to itself, so each frequency is adjusted on its own,

143
00:08:32,718 --> 00:08:35,222
like the sliders on an audio equalizer.

144
00:08:35,262 --> 00:08:38,391
That's the block-diagonal structure drawn in the paper.

145
00:08:39,247 --> 00:08:40,944
The second knob is the scale.

146
00:08:40,984 --> 00:08:44,513
The code divides by n, then multiplies by a tuned scale:

147
00:08:44,553 --> 00:08:47,677
ten for RTE, fifty for MRPC.

148
00:08:47,717 --> 00:08:53,287
Because B never stretches anything, the size of ΔW is exactly scale over n,

149
00:08:53,327 --> 00:08:55,674
times the size of the coefficient vector.

150
00:08:55,714 --> 00:08:58,509
So the scale sets how hard the coefficients push,

151
00:08:58,549 --> 00:09:03,078
and it trades off against the learning rate, the step size of each training update.

152
00:09:03,118 --> 00:09:07,952
Learning rate times scale comes out similar for both tasks: between three and four.

153
00:09:08,814 --> 00:09:10,006
One more detail.

154
00:09:10,046 --> 00:09:13,545
In the GLUE runs, the coefficients start as random numbers,

155
00:09:13,585 --> 00:09:17,937
not at zero like LoRA, so training begins from a small random nudge.

156
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:20,672
Now into the real model.

157
00:09:20,712 --> 00:09:22,722
RoBERTa-base has twelve layers.

158
00:09:22,762 --> 00:09:26,789
In each one, FrameFT wraps two matrices in the attention block:

159
00:09:26,829 --> 00:09:28,939
the query and value projections.

160
00:09:28,979 --> 00:09:32,871
Roughly, the query decides what each word looks for in the other words,

161
00:09:32,911 --> 00:09:35,957
and the value decides what information it passes along.

162
00:09:35,997 --> 00:09:37,892
Everything original is frozen.

163
00:09:37,932 --> 00:09:42,303
The only trainable pieces are the thousand coefficients per wrapped matrix,

164
00:09:42,343 --> 00:09:45,028
plus a small classification head on top.

165
00:09:45,880 --> 00:09:49,434
Each time the model reads an input, called the forward pass,

166
00:09:49,474 --> 00:09:54,296
the layer computes its usual output and adds the input times ΔW,

167
00:09:54,336 --> 00:09:56,402
rebuilt from the current coefficients.

168
00:09:56,442 --> 00:09:59,734
On the backward pass, the gradient arrives: for every weight,

169
00:09:59,774 --> 00:10:02,035
which way it should move to reduce the error.

170
00:10:02,075 --> 00:10:04,126
Each coefficient gets its share:

171
00:10:04,166 --> 00:10:07,351
the part of that gradient that lines up with its own pattern.

172
00:10:07,391 --> 00:10:11,764
The optimizer uses two learning rates: a large one for the coefficients,

173
00:10:11,804 --> 00:10:15,292
which are heavily scaled down, and a smaller one for the head.

174
00:10:16,147 --> 00:10:18,430
Two engineering choices keep this cheap.

175
00:10:18,470 --> 00:10:21,157
Each basis is built once per size and cached,

176
00:10:21,197 --> 00:10:24,620
with one copy per GPU, shared by every layer.

177
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:28,684
And after training, ΔW can be merged straight into W,

178
00:10:28,724 --> 00:10:32,647
so the fine-tuned model runs exactly as fast as the original.

179
00:10:33,514 --> 00:10:36,519
Count it up: twelve layers, times two matrices,

180
00:10:36,559 --> 00:10:40,221
times a thousand coefficients, is 24,000 numbers.

181
00:10:40,261 --> 00:10:43,286
LoRA, at rank eight, needs about 300,000.

182
00:10:43,326 --> 00:10:46,624
Full fine-tuning needs 125 million.

183
00:10:46,664 --> 00:10:52,360
Stored as 32-bit numbers, a FrameFT adapter is about 96 kilobytes.

184
00:10:53,714 --> 00:10:54,712
Does it work?

185
00:10:54,752 --> 00:10:59,612
The paper tests on GLUE, a standard set of language-understanding tasks,

186
00:10:59,652 --> 00:11:02,650
like deciding whether one sentence implies another,

187
00:11:02,690 --> 00:11:05,312
or whether two sentences say the same thing.

188
00:11:05,352 --> 00:11:09,630
On RoBERTa-base, FrameFT averages 86.1,

189
00:11:09,670 --> 00:11:13,755
compared with 85.2 for both LoRA and full fine-tuning,

190
00:11:13,795 --> 00:11:19,709
while training 24,000 numbers instead of 300,000 or 125 million.

191
00:11:20,547 --> 00:11:25,258
The pattern holds when instruction-tuning Llama 2, with 7 billion parameters,

192
00:11:25,298 --> 00:11:26,933
and on vision transformers,

193
00:11:26,973 --> 00:11:32,396
where FrameFT beats LoRA and FourierFT while training fewer numbers than either.

194
00:11:33,647 --> 00:11:35,719
Now, the experiment we're running.

195
00:11:35,759 --> 00:11:39,205
At these settings, the frame is just an orthogonal matrix.

196
00:11:39,245 --> 00:11:43,218
So does its wave structure matter, or would any orthogonal basis do?

197
00:11:43,258 --> 00:11:46,525
We swap B for two alternatives, and change nothing else.

198
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:50,429
The random arm uses a uniformly random rotation,

199
00:11:50,469 --> 00:11:53,416
made by taking a grid of random numbers and straightening

200
00:11:53,456 --> 00:11:57,239
it into an orthogonal matrix with a QR decomposition.

201
00:11:57,279 --> 00:12:00,197
The identity arm uses the plainest basis of all,

202
00:12:00,237 --> 00:12:03,357
where each coefficient changes exactly one weight.

203
00:12:03,514 --> 00:12:06,997
To keep it fair, all three share the same coefficient positions,

204
00:12:07,037 --> 00:12:09,369
seeds, data order, and settings.

205
00:12:09,409 --> 00:12:13,560
And orthogonality gives a neat guarantee: with the same coefficients,

206
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:18,735
all three updates have exactly the same size, rank, and singular values,

207
00:12:18,775 --> 00:12:22,760
which measure how strongly a matrix stretches along its main directions.

208
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:26,899
They differ only in orientation: which input directions they read,

209
00:12:26,939 --> 00:12:29,065
and which output directions they write.

210
00:12:29,914 --> 00:12:31,237
Results so far.

211
00:12:31,277 --> 00:12:36,533
On RTE, with three seeds each, meaning three repeat runs with different randomness,

212
00:12:36,573 --> 00:12:41,395
frame and random are neck and neck at their best epoch, around 79.5,

213
00:12:41,435 --> 00:12:45,447
right at the paper's number, while identity trails by seven points.

214
00:12:45,487 --> 00:12:48,976
At the final epoch, frame leads random by about two points.

215
00:12:49,016 --> 00:12:53,788
On MRPC, with fewer seeds, the order is frame, then random, then identity.

216
00:12:53,828 --> 00:12:58,301
And all three reach similar training loss, so identity isn't failing to learn.

217
00:12:58,341 --> 00:13:00,896
It learns something that generalizes worse.

218
00:13:01,747 --> 00:13:06,480
One likely reason: with a thousand single entries scattered over the matrix,

219
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:10,268
about a quarter of the input features never touch the update at all.

220
00:13:10,308 --> 00:13:14,290
Dense bases give every coefficient a say over the whole matrix.

221
00:13:14,330 --> 00:13:16,948
So spreading coefficients out clearly matters.

222
00:13:16,988 --> 00:13:21,998
Whether the frame's particular waves beat a random rotation is a smaller effect.

223
00:13:22,038 --> 00:13:23,609
With two or three seeds,

224
00:13:23,649 --> 00:13:27,284
and a validation set where one example is worth a third of a point,

225
00:13:27,324 --> 00:13:29,197
it needs more seeds to settle.

226
00:13:30,647 --> 00:13:32,515
Let's recap the whole pipeline.

227
00:13:32,555 --> 00:13:36,559
From two numbers, n and l, Spectral Tetris, modulation,

228
00:13:36,599 --> 00:13:38,809
and a real-valued rewrite build a frozen,

229
00:13:38,849 --> 00:13:42,772
orthogonal frame B, shared by every layer of that size.

230
00:13:42,812 --> 00:13:46,653
From a seed, a thousand positions are picked in a sparse matrix S,

231
00:13:46,693 --> 00:13:48,879
and only those coefficients are trained.

232
00:13:48,919 --> 00:13:54,305
The update is scale over n, times B transpose, S, B: measure, edit, rebuild.

233
00:13:54,345 --> 00:13:58,114
It's added to the frozen weights, and merged in when training is done.

234
00:13:58,980 --> 00:14:02,534
Twenty-four thousand numbers, a couple of seeds, and a formula.

235
00:14:02,574 --> 00:14:04,034
That's the whole adapter.

236
00:14:04,074 --> 00:14:05,368
Thanks for watching.

